title: $:/plugins/btheado/notebook-kit/readme

This plugin provides a `<$notebook-kit/>` widget that renders TiddlyWiki
tiddlers as cells in an [[Observable Notebook Kit|https://observablehq.com/notebook-kit/]]
notebook. The [[notebooks 2.0 system guide|https://new.observablehq.com/@observablehq/notebooks-2-0-system-guide]] explains about writing notebook cells.

To create a new notebook click this button: {{$:/plugins/btheado/notebook-kit/ui/CreateNotebookButton}}

Or if you want to do it manaully, then add the following transclusion to a tiddler:

```
{{||$:/plugins/btheado/notebook-kit/NotebookStoryTemplate}}
```

This template wraps the `<$notebook-kit/>` widget with a story river, allowing
the notebook's cells to be edited in place while viewing the notebook.

The content of the `<$notebook-kit/>` widget completely determines the
notebook's layout. After the widget has rendered, it scans the generated DOM
for elements that identify notebook cells. Each matching element specifies the
tiddler that should be evaluated as a notebook cell.

An element with the `tc-notebook-output` class is treated as a notebook cell.
The title of the cell tiddler is taken from its `data-tiddler-title` attribute.

For example:

```wiki
<$notebook-kit>
<div class="tc-notebook-output" data-tiddler-title="mynotebook/cell1"/>
<div class="tc-notebook-output" data-tiddler-title="mynotebook/cell2"/>
</$notebook-kit>
```

In this example, the cell tiddlers are displayed as notebook output but cannot
be edited from within the notebook. They are still evaluated and rendered as
notebook cells.

!! Cell type mappings

The cell's `type` field selects its Notebook Kit language mode. Mappings are
ordinary configuration tiddlers below `$:/config/NotebookKitPlugin/TypeMappings/`.
For example, create `$:/config/NotebookKitPlugin/TypeMappings/text/x-python`
with the text `python` to evaluate `text/x-python` cells in Python mode. Each
added cell type requires a JavaScript tagged template literal function whose
name matches the mapped mode (in this example, `python`). The plugin supplies
defaults for JavaScript, Observable JavaScript, TypeScript, Markdown, HTML,
SQL, SVG, TeX, and Graphviz. Unmapped cell types are not transpiled or
evaluated.

!! Tiddler attachments

Notebook cells can use any tiddler as an Observable `FileAttachment`; the
attachment name is the tiddler's exact title. The tiddler's `text` field is
the file contents and its `type` field becomes the MIME type. For example, a
tiddler named `data/points.csv` with `type: text/csv` can be read from a
JavaScript cell with:

```js
points = FileAttachment("data/points.csv").csv({typed: true})
```

Text tiddlers are encoded as UTF-8. A tiddler with `encoding: base64` is
decoded first, allowing binary attachments such as images to be stored as
base64 text. Attachment URLs are local Blob URLs, so they work offline once
Notebook Kit itself has loaded and do not expose a network endpoint.
When an already-used attachment tiddler changes, the notebook re-runs so
dependent cells read the new contents.

!! Tiddler JavaScript modules

JavaScript tiddlers can be imported as ES modules with the `tw:` prefix. The
text field of the named tiddler is used as the module source, so it must use
standard ES module exports:

```js
// tiddler title: my-library
export function greeting(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
```

```js
import {greeting} from "tw:my-library";
display(greeting("Ada"));
```

Other import specifiers, including `npm:` and HTTPS URLs, continue to use
Notebook Kit's default resolver. When an imported tiddler changes, notebook
cells are re-evaluated. Imported tiddler modules should currently be
self-contained: imports inside a `tw:` module are handled by the browser and
are not themselves rewritten by Notebook Kit.

!! Tiddler notebook imports

Use the `tw-notebook:` prefix to import reactive variables from a collection
of notebook cell tiddlers. Everything after the prefix is a TiddlyWiki filter:

```js
import {base, doubled as result}
  from "tw-notebook:[tag[shared-notebook-cell]]";
```

Put the import declaration in its own cell. Other cells in the importing
notebook can then refer to `base` and `result` normally. The filter selects the
source cells; the named imports select variables declared by those cells. All
selected cells are evaluated together in a separate reactive module, allowing
them to depend on each other. Aliases such as `doubled as result` are supported.

The filter is evaluated with the importing `<$notebook-kit>` widget as its
variable context. When a selected cell changes, its imported variables and
their dependents update reactively. The filter is also evaluated again after
wiki changes, so cells that newly match or cease to match are added or removed
from the imported module.

!! Notes

I highly recommend installing the Codemirror and Highlight plugins. Their syntax
highlighting of source code is very useful.

''NOTE'': The notebook-kit library is not bundled with this plugin. It is loaded
from a CDN on the internet. Therefore, this plugin is not offline capable.

''CAUTION'': Tiddlywiki does not allow script tags. Nor does it execute
javascript tiddlers which are dragged into the wiki until the wiki is reloaded.
Whatever security protections that provides is lost if you install this
notebook-kit plugin as it allows immediate execution arbitrary javascript.
Proceed with caution when using this plugin.

!! Installation

To install this plugin into your own wiki, drag and drop this link into your
wiki: [[$:/plugins/btheado/notebook-kit]] and reload the wiki.
